{"parentUuid":"95ad587c-e257-44de-931c-b8c2e9ff9532","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdBipyt7L4ThvDuyMbpri","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Confirmed — token watcher matches (−5.4% real / −6.2% cost, n=14). Nothing new; opus head-to-head is complete.\n\nRecording the opus finding (durable — confirms the pattern on a frontier model):"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":543,"cache_read_input_tokens":709809,"output_tokens":1145,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":543,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1145,"cache_read_input_tokens":709809,"cache_creation_input_tokens":543,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":543},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdBiptc7H9WXyVk7hh2F6","type":"assistant","uuid":"4f464f07-f421-4f92-be1d-70b8de7408d3","timestamp":"2026-07-19T16:21:06.501Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"de272aad-c620-4d71-bbdd-27dd2d3dc8af","version":"2.1.201","gitBranch":"main","slug":"quizzical-sniffing-quasar"}
{"parentUuid":"4f464f07-f421-4f92-be1d-70b8de7408d3","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdBipyt7L4ThvDuyMbpri","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0155Ntr5e8pLqZQ6btHnS5Pb","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/suhaan/.claude/projects/-Users-suhaan-devenv/memory/graph-swebench-verdict.md","old_string":"**Bottom line to tell the user:**","new_string":"**OPUS head-to-head (2026-07-19, 15 tasks sampled evenly from the OFFICIAL 300, not the pilot; 13 evaluated — redis/tokio docker images won't build locally):** baseline(no graph) 11/13 = **85%**, treated(+graph) 10/13 = **77%**, graph accuracy Δ **−1** (0 graph wins, 1 regression rubocop = noise). Tokens: graph **−5.4% real / −6.2% cost** (smaller than haiku-naive −23% because opus navigates efficiently in smart mode). So the pattern holds on a FRONTIER model too: **graph = token/navigation win, accuracy-neutral.** Opus baseline 85% already clears its published 84.4% + Sonnet 5's 78.3% on its own — it's a strong coder; the graph just makes it ~5-6% cheaper at equal accuracy. NOTE on eval infra: a random 300-sample needs docker images BUILT locally (`--namespace none`); default pulls from swebench dockerhub → 404 for unpublished tasks; and Docker Desktop VM disk fills fast (prune between builds). The 300-sampled task file is `opus15_tasks.json`; harness now reads `TASKS_FILE` env.\n\n**Bottom line to tell the user:**"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":543,"cache_read_input_tokens":709809,"output_tokens":1145,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":543,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1145,"cache_read_input_tokens":709809,"cache_creation_input_tokens":543,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":543},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdBiptc7H9WXyVk7hh2F6","type":"assistant","uuid":"95b68ea6-2492-4077-a838-4c7bec075a9d","timestamp":"2026-07-19T16:21:13.836Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"de272aad-c620-4d71-bbdd-27dd2d3dc8af","version":"2.1.201","gitBranch":"main","slug":"quizzical-sniffing-quasar"}
{"parentUuid":"95b68ea6-2492-4077-a838-4c7bec075a9d","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"ecd7ca2e-0da8-45e5-94df-5b1fbd97a108","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0155Ntr5e8pLqZQ6btHnS5Pb","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/suhaan/.claude/projects/-Users-suhaan-devenv/memory/graph-swebench-verdict.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"871ab786-dee2-4a58-a756-254a64dd7974","timestamp":"2026-07-19T16:21:13.864Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/suhaan/.claude/projects/-Users-suhaan-devenv/memory/graph-swebench-verdict.md","oldString":"**Bottom line to tell the user:**","newString":"**OPUS head-to-head (2026-07-19, 15 tasks sampled evenly from the OFFICIAL 300, not the pilot; 13 evaluated — redis/tokio docker images won't build locally):** baseline(no graph) 11/13 = **85%**, treated(+graph) 10/13 = **77%**, graph accuracy Δ **−1** (0 graph wins, 1 regression rubocop = noise). Tokens: graph **−5.4% real / −6.2% cost** (smaller than haiku-naive −23% because opus navigates efficiently in smart mode). So the pattern holds on a FRONTIER model too: **graph = token/navigation win, accuracy-neutral.** Opus baseline 85% already clears its published 84.4% + Sonnet 5's 78.3% on its own — it's a strong coder; the graph just makes it ~5-6% cheaper at equal accuracy. NOTE on eval infra: a random 300-sample needs docker images BUILT locally (`--namespace none`); default pulls from swebench dockerhub → 404 for unpublished tasks; and Docker Desktop VM disk fills fast (prune between builds). The 300-sampled task file is `opus15_tasks.json`; harness now reads `TASKS_FILE` env.\n\n**Bottom line to tell the user:**","originalFile":"---\nname: graph-swebench-verdict\ndescription: \"agentic-swebench measured verdict — graph is a token win (-19-23%), NOT an accuracy lever (~0, noise-band); tests-first enrichment backfired\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: de272aad-c620-4d71-bbdd-27dd2d3dc8af\n---\n\nMeasured (not predicted) verdict of the entire-graph agentic-swebench benchmark (haiku, 30-task pilot, SWE-bench Multilingual), `~/Documents/Coding/brain-swebench`. Settles the long \"make the graph win >20% tokens AND significantly-more accuracy\" goal.\n\n**Tokens — graph WINS (lean graph = def/callers/callees):** vs a whole-file-naive baseline (faithful mini-swe-agent, = Thomas's 40% setup): **−19% $cost, −23% total tokens, −12.4% real tokens.** The measurement was the issue (user's \"99.99% harness\" point): an agent-picks-its-own-reads naive proxy gave only −11% (too smart); forcing whole-file `cat` (mini-swe-agent's actual behavior) gives −23%. Smart Claude Code baseline understates the graph (−1%). Per-lookup deterministic = −93%.\n\n**Accuracy — ~0, noise-band, NOT movable via the graph:** two full 30-runs gave **+1 and −3** (baseline 15→t16; baseline 17→t14). haiku per-task variance is ±100%, so ±3/30 is noise. The graph helps you FIND code, not WRITE fixes — solve-rate is coding-ability-bound. Proven structurally AND empirically.\n\n**Last-resort graph enrichment BACKFIRED (measured):** added `tests <sym>` (covering tests + source) / `impls <sym>` queries + a \"read the covering tests before editing\" prompt. Result: tokens −23%→−4% (agent over-reads test bodies) AND accuracy −3 (overfits to shown tests → regressions on hidden tests). **Reverted** the prompt push; kept `tests`/`impls` as optional `graph-query` commands (they're correct, just not routine). The lean graph was already the better tool.\n\n**Budget-regime test (40-turn cap, cheap-deployment) ALSO failed both:** accuracy baseline 10 → treated 9 (−1, noise); tokens +2.1% (treated worse — at a tight cap both arms truncate before the graph's read-avoidance accrues, and gq query overhead shows). So the token win is regime-specific: it needs a FULL session + naive baseline; under a budget or with enrichment it evaporates.\n\n**Brief lever (round-trip collapse) — built, measured, net-negative for haiku, REVERTED (injection off; `gq brief` kept as a tool):** auto-injecting a task-scoped packet (top issue-relevant symbols + source, ranked) up front to skip iterative navigation. brief-6: AGG real **+19.6%** (worse). Two failure modes: (1) string-match ranking is noisy (fmt got printf *headers*, nushell/redis matched generic words) — precise text→code needs embeddings, i.e. the BRAIN's vector search; `SIMILAR_TO` edges are code→code, don't help text→code; (2) even a CORRECT brief doesn't stop haiku exploring on a complex fix (fmt: right brief, still gq=13 / 17→71 turns). It only won when the brief was precise AND the fix simple: **druid −42%, immutable −30%** — proof the mechanism CAN clear −40%, gated on brief precision + agent compliance.\n\n**Architecture conclusion (vindicates graph+brain):** graph-ALONE tops out at −23% (lean); pushing to −40% needs **precise briefs = the brain's semantic (vector) ranking on top of the graph** (druid proves −42% with a precise brief) AND a compliant agent. So Thomas's 40% \"fix completely,\" if it's a context/brief result, is a graph+brain number, not graph-alone. The graph is the structural substrate; the brain supplies text→code relevance.\n\n**v2 precise brief (file:line + code-fence + dep-exclusion ranking) — regressed at N=30 on BOTH axes:** looked great on a 6-task pick (real −24%, redis −52%/nushell −41%) but that sample was winner-biased. Full N=30: **real −8.7% / cost −14.5% / total −18.9% — WORSE than lean on every metric** (injection cache-read overhead + complex-fix draggers). Accuracy **+0/30** (baseline 15 → treated 15; 2 wins druid/gson ± 2 regressions fmt/hugo = variance). So accuracy is now flat across **6 configs** (100t +1/−3, enriched −3, budget −1, sonnet −1, v2-brief +0) — even a PRECISE brief that lands the agent on the exact code (gq=0) doesn't lift resolved%, because finding≠fixing. Same small-sample-optimism → N=30-regression pattern as every enrichment. **Lean graph (−19% cost / −23% total, accuracy-flat) is the robust ceiling; additions consistently make it worse.** Canonical 30-task baseline frozen at `results/haiku_baseline_canonical` (15/30 resolved) so treated-only experiments stop re-running it. BRIEF_INJECT gated OFF by default.\n\n**Harness-suppression correction (2026-07-19) — accuracy WAS being under-measured, then hit a real wall at 60%:** the flat-accuracy runs used a crippled harness (default effort, one-shot \"just stop\", no verify). Deep audit found it: haiku near-misses (28/30 patches in the SAME file as gold — navigates right, fails on a detail/completeness). Fixing the harness climbs it, clean pass@1: naive one-shot 15/30 (50%) → smart+VERIFY(TDD iteration) 16/30 (53%) → smart+graph+VERIFY+effort=high 18/30 (60%) → effort=max 18/30 (60%, PLATEAU, two independent clean runs agree). So the harness was suppressing ~10pt (real, recovered) and haiku now beats its published Multilingual (~50%). **But 60% is the honest pass@1 ceiling; Sonnet 5 = 78.3% pass@1 is +18pt beyond it — a model-tier wall.** IMPORTANT integrity note: re-running ONLY the failures and keeping passes gave a spurious \"70%\" — that's pass@k / eval-selection, NOT pass@1; refused to report it. Opus (84.4%) is the only honest path >78.3%. New harness levers added: `VERIFY` (TDD iteration prompt) + `CC_EFFORT` (extended-thinking) in run_cc_one.sh, `gq similar`/`brief` in graph-query.\n\n**Smart-baseline accuracy test (the \"right\" accuracy comparison) — ALSO flat:** hypothesis: 6 flat configs all used a whole-file-naive baseline that reads EVERYTHING → zero accuracy headroom (graph can't help you find code you already read); a SELECTIVE (Read/Grep) baseline can miss scattered code → graph's precise refs could rescue incomplete fixes. Tested smart baseline vs smart+graph on 8 baseline-failed tasks: **baseline 1/8, treated 1/8 = +0** (ripgrep won, bat regressed). So accuracy is flat against BOTH baseline types — the failures aren't from missing code (grep finds it), they're from writing the wrong fix (coding). **7 accuracy configs total, all ~0.** finding≠fixing is structural, not a baseline artifact.\n\n**How it was reached without cheating:** 3 models × 2 baselines × 5 harness levers × median-of-3 × per-lookup × enrichment × budget × brief-v1/v2 × smart-baseline-accuracy. Accuracy measured ~0 in **7 configs** (100t +1/−3, enriched −3, budget −1, sonnet −1, v2-brief +0, smart-baseline +0). Refused to inflate the baseline beyond faithful-mini-swe-agent, cherry-pick runs, or tune-to-target. See [[cost-analytics-state]], [[orchestrator-architecture]].\n\n**Bottom line to tell the user:** the graph is a real **token/navigation win (−19-23%)**, not an accuracy booster. The strict \">20% tokens AND significantly-more accuracy\" is half-achievable on the subscription: tokens yes, accuracy no (structural, not an optimization gap). The definitive accuracy number needs the real mini-swe-agent (ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, verified unconfigured) or Thomas's exact test definition.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":29,"oldLines":4,"newStart":29,"newLines":6,"lines":[" "," **How it was reached without cheating:** 3 models × 2 baselines × 5 harness levers × median-of-3 × per-lookup × enrichment × budget × brief-v1/v2 × smart-baseline-accuracy. Accuracy measured ~0 in **7 configs** (100t +1/−3, enriched −3, budget −1, sonnet −1, v2-brief +0, smart-baseline +0). Refused to inflate the baseline beyond faithful-mini-swe-agent, cherry-pick runs, or tune-to-target. See [[cost-analytics-state]], [[orchestrator-architecture]]."," ","+**OPUS head-to-head (2026-07-19, 15 tasks sampled evenly from the OFFICIAL 300, not the pilot; 13 evaluated — redis/tokio docker images won't build locally):** baseline(no graph) 11/13 = **85%**, treated(+graph) 10/13 = **77%**, graph accuracy Δ **−1** (0 graph wins, 1 regression rubocop = noise). Tokens: graph **−5.4% real / −6.2% cost** (smaller than haiku-naive −23% because opus navigates efficiently in smart mode). So the pattern holds on a FRONTIER model too: **graph = token/navigation win, accuracy-neutral.** Opus baseline 85% already clears its published 84.4% + Sonnet 5's 78.3% on its own — it's a strong coder; the graph just makes it ~5-6% cheaper at equal accuracy. NOTE on eval infra: a random 300-sample needs docker images BUILT locally (`--namespace none`); default pulls from swebench dockerhub → 404 for unpublished tasks; and Docker Desktop VM disk fills fast (prune between builds). The 300-sampled task file is `opus15_tasks.json`; harness now reads `TASKS_FILE` env.","+"," **Bottom line to tell the user:** the graph is a real **token/navigation win (−19-23%)**, not an accuracy booster. The strict \">20% tokens AND significantly-more accuracy\" is half-achievable on the subscription: tokens yes, accuracy no (structural, not an optimization gap). The definitive accuracy number needs the real mini-swe-agent (ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, verified unconfigured) or Thomas's exact test definition."]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"95b68ea6-2492-4077-a838-4c7bec075a9d","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"de272aad-c620-4d71-bbdd-27dd2d3dc8af","version":"2.1.201","gitBranch":"main","slug":"quizzical-sniffing-quasar"}
